WEBVTT

00:00:00.975 --> 00:00:06.500
Welcome to Day 2: Force Multiplier, Leveraging AI and Email Management.

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:10.225
We'll start with the executive's exposure and board communication,

00:00:10.225 --> 00:00:14.450
then move into the force multiplier mindset, cognitive load and systems,

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:17.888
and what being a force multiplier means in legal work.

00:00:17.888 --> 00:00:21.075
After that, we'll look at using AI with precision,

00:00:21.075 --> 00:00:23.525
and we'll finish with email management.

00:00:24.275 --> 00:00:28.300
By the end of today, you'll be ready to manage the executive's exposure,

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:32.263
prepare board and investor communications to a higher standard,

00:00:32.263 --> 00:00:37.500
use AI tools with good judgment, and run the inbox as a control system.

00:00:39.425 --> 00:00:44.175
Section 1 of 6: Executive Exposure and Board Communication.

00:00:44.175 --> 00:00:47.387
We'll cover managing constant executive exposure,

00:00:47.387 --> 00:00:50.675
board updates, and preparing investor briefings.

00:00:52.575 --> 00:00:57.125
Part of our job is spotting risk hiding inside things that look routine.

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:01.988
A senior executive is exposed all the time: to legal risk,

00:01:01.988 --> 00:01:06.775
regulation, public opinion, investors and the firm's reputation.

00:01:06.775 --> 00:01:10.075
Most of that risk doesn't arrive labelled 'dangerous'.

00:01:10.075 --> 00:01:14.925
It turns up as an ordinary email, a friendly invitation or a contract someone wants

00:01:14.925 --> 00:01:16.100
signed today.

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:20.200
We're often the first to see it, which makes us the first line of defence.

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:26.000
First, we flag risky emails before they're sent, not after.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:29.512
Next, we check invitations for anything that could look bad.

00:01:29.512 --> 00:01:34.363
Then, contracts get properly reviewed, never signed on a quick summary.

00:01:34.363 --> 00:01:37.700
After that, we watch compliance deadlines ourselves.

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:42.025
Finally, we handle sensitive messages with discretion by default.

00:01:42.775 --> 00:01:44.462
Here's one to think about.

00:01:44.462 --> 00:01:49.338
Elias is invited to speak on a panel sponsored by a company on the other side of one

00:01:49.338 --> 00:01:51.375
of the firm's active cases.

00:01:51.375 --> 00:01:53.725
It arrives as a routine invite.

00:01:53.725 --> 00:01:54.825
What do you do?

00:01:55.625 --> 00:01:59.475
Or a friendly vendor asks for a photo of Elias with their product for their

00:01:59.475 --> 00:02:00.575
newsletter.

00:02:00.575 --> 00:02:02.038
It seems harmless.

00:02:02.038 --> 00:02:04.025
What exposure could it create?

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:09.325
A board update isn't just a formal status email.

00:02:09.325 --> 00:02:13.700
Board members may read it weeks later, or look back at it if something goes wrong,

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:17.975
so it needs more precision and a bit more context than an internal note.

00:02:17.975 --> 00:02:23.125
Same structure as always: what's happening, what it means and what we recommend.

00:02:23.125 --> 00:02:26.225
We just give enough background to understand it cold.

00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:31.475
First, we confirm exactly who'll receive it, because outside directors need

00:02:31.475 --> 00:02:34.125
something different from the internal team.

00:02:34.125 --> 00:02:38.188
Next, we lead with what matters for oversight: a decision needed,

00:02:38.188 --> 00:02:40.275
a risk or a milestone.

00:02:40.275 --> 00:02:44.700
Finally, it gets a second pair of eyes, however routine it seems.

00:02:45.475 --> 00:02:46.600
Think about this.

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:50.738
You're drafting a board update on a project that's six weeks behind.

00:02:50.738 --> 00:02:54.950
How do you give an honest picture without downplaying it or causing panic?

00:02:55.725 --> 00:02:59.950
And if a board member emails you directly asking for 'the real story',

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:04.550
outside the formal update, how do you respond without a governance mistake?

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:10.662
Investors judge both what's in the briefing and how well it was prepared.

00:03:10.662 --> 00:03:14.575
Every number will be tested, and so will whoever prepared it.

00:03:14.575 --> 00:03:18.712
Our job is to make sure the executive walks in with the material condensed and

00:03:18.712 --> 00:03:21.700
checked, not a pile of raw documents.

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:26.400
First, we confirm what the briefing should cover and in what format,

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:29.625
because investors often expect a set template.

00:03:29.625 --> 00:03:34.100
Next, we take every figure from verified, original sources.

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:38.200
Finally, we give the executive a heads-up on anything likely to draw a tough

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:39.300
question.

00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:41.775
Take a second with this one.

00:03:41.775 --> 00:03:45.750
One figure you were given doesn't match the firm's own recent report.

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:48.175
What do you do before the materials go out?

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:52.975
And if an investor asks for 'the pipeline numbers in advance',

00:03:52.975 --> 00:03:55.875
and Elias hasn't approved sharing anything early,

00:03:55.875 --> 00:03:57.250
what do you reply?

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:03.000
Section 2 of 6: The Force Multiplier Mindset.

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:05.925
We'll look at the shift from helper to force multiplier,

00:04:05.925 --> 00:04:10.000
what that autonomy is and isn't, and how to measure the change.

00:04:11.950 --> 00:04:13.862
A helper does what they're told.

00:04:13.862 --> 00:04:17.275
A force multiplier already has the next answer ready.

00:04:18.025 --> 00:04:20.550
The executive asks us to book a room.

00:04:20.550 --> 00:04:22.275
The helper books the room.

00:04:22.275 --> 00:04:25.650
The force multiplier books the room, sends the invites,

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:29.188
prints the agenda and checks whether the client needs parking.

00:04:29.188 --> 00:04:30.800
That's not overstepping.

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:34.825
It's thinking one step ahead, and having it ready before he asks.

00:04:36.775 --> 00:04:40.550
First, before a routine task, we ask what he'll probably need next,

00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:42.250
and prepare that too.

00:04:42.250 --> 00:04:45.825
Then, we build the habit through steady, reliable work.

00:04:45.825 --> 00:04:49.050
It grows over time; it doesn't switch on overnight.

00:04:50.975 --> 00:04:53.750
After that, we watch for requests that repeat.

00:04:53.750 --> 00:04:57.450
By the second or third time, we should be anticipating them.

00:04:57.450 --> 00:05:01.738
Finally, we check ourselves now and then: are we just finishing tasks,

00:05:01.738 --> 00:05:04.000
or arriving with the next step done?

00:05:04.775 --> 00:05:06.900
Here's a situation to work through.

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:11.750
For the third week in a row, Elias asks on Monday morning for 'the list of what's

00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:16.225
due this week.' What would a force multiplier do before next Monday?

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:21.600
The biggest change in this role happens in how we see ourselves,

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:23.225
not in our job title.

00:05:23.975 --> 00:05:27.238
The helper looks for approval, avoids owning decisions,

00:05:27.238 --> 00:05:31.562
waits for instructions and measures success by how fast they reply.

00:05:31.562 --> 00:05:35.325
The force multiplier owns the outcome, sets up the decision,

00:05:35.325 --> 00:05:39.475
understands how the executive thinks and measures success by how much more he gets

00:05:39.475 --> 00:05:40.438
done.

00:05:40.438 --> 00:05:44.675
Same person, same job; a very different way of showing up.

00:05:45.450 --> 00:05:48.500
So when a request is unclear, notice your default.

00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:53.500
Do you ask 'What should I do?', or suggest an approach and ask for a quick OK?

00:05:53.500 --> 00:05:57.425
And practise attaching a suggestion to every fact you pass on.

00:05:58.225 --> 00:06:01.575
Ask yourself: which mindset describes you right now,

00:06:01.575 --> 00:06:04.725
and which helper habit will you start replacing this week?

00:06:06.675 --> 00:06:11.625
In today's executive world, an assistant who only helps becomes a bottleneck.

00:06:11.625 --> 00:06:15.550
It isn't a personal failing; the job has simply outgrown it.

00:06:16.325 --> 00:06:21.650
Executives work fast, in public, with legal exposure and money on the line.

00:06:21.650 --> 00:06:26.762
If every decision waits for step-by-step instructions, we slow everything down.

00:06:26.762 --> 00:06:29.850
A force multiplier becomes part of the machinery.

00:06:30.625 --> 00:06:33.850
First, we look for signs we're still in helper mode:

00:06:33.850 --> 00:06:36.938
waiting for instructions on things we've done before,

00:06:36.938 --> 00:06:40.950
or asking permission for decisions we've already shown we can make.

00:06:40.950 --> 00:06:46.150
Finally, we change it on purpose: the next familiar request gets a recommendation

00:06:46.150 --> 00:06:47.250
attached.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:49.312
Consider this.

00:06:49.312 --> 00:06:53.688
A colleague prides herself on answering Elias's messages at midnight.

00:06:53.688 --> 00:06:56.888
She's exhausted, and her daytime work is slipping.

00:06:56.888 --> 00:06:59.125
What's the better way to add value?

00:07:01.025 --> 00:07:03.375
Picture the day before a big pitch.

00:07:03.375 --> 00:07:07.212
The client suddenly adds requests involving three departments.

00:07:07.212 --> 00:07:09.912
A helper forwards the emails and hopes.

00:07:09.912 --> 00:07:14.675
A force multiplier works through it in three steps, always in the same order.

00:07:15.475 --> 00:07:18.600
First, we prioritise: what's most time-sensitive,

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:21.775
what's waiting on something else, and what the options are.

00:07:21.775 --> 00:07:25.675
Next, we coordinate: everyone on a shared board or document,

00:07:25.675 --> 00:07:29.950
with automatic reminders, so nobody's working from an old email.

00:07:29.950 --> 00:07:33.450
Finally, we close the loop: every task gets an owner,

00:07:33.450 --> 00:07:37.875
it all comes together in one report, and we confirm the client received it.

00:07:38.700 --> 00:07:40.000
Now try this.

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:43.275
It's 6 p.m. Finance and marketing have delivered,

00:07:43.275 --> 00:07:46.450
but legal's conflicts confirmation hasn't arrived,

00:07:46.450 --> 00:07:49.300
and the pitch is at 9 a.m. What do you do tonight,

00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:51.100
and what do you tell Elias?

00:07:53.050 --> 00:07:55.550
Autonomy is earned and agreed in advance.

00:07:55.550 --> 00:07:57.525
It's never simply assumed.

00:07:58.300 --> 00:08:01.138
Being a force multiplier doesn't mean overstepping,

00:08:01.138 --> 00:08:03.925
acting like the boss or going off on our own.

00:08:03.925 --> 00:08:06.512
It means freedom that's been agreed in advance,

00:08:06.512 --> 00:08:09.138
thinking ahead and carrying things out well.

00:08:09.138 --> 00:08:11.475
Confidence that knows its limits.

00:08:12.250 --> 00:08:14.900
First, before acting on our own on something new,

00:08:14.900 --> 00:08:18.525
we confirm it's inside the limits we've actually been given.

00:08:18.525 --> 00:08:22.200
Finally, if we're unsure whether something is ours to decide,

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:24.000
that doubt is a signal.

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:25.250
Check first.

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:27.463
Take a second with this one.

00:08:27.463 --> 00:08:31.025
A partner tells you, 'Elias always lets you approve these,

00:08:31.025 --> 00:08:36.525
just sign off on the $2,000 expense.' Your written limit is $500.

00:08:36.525 --> 00:08:37.725
What do you say?

00:08:39.675 --> 00:08:44.250
'I cut response time from two days to four hours' lands very differently from 'I'm

00:08:44.250 --> 00:08:45.375
doing well.'

00:08:46.125 --> 00:08:49.138
The good news is this kind of impact can be measured:

00:08:49.138 --> 00:08:52.688
a smaller inbox, more of his time on important work,

00:08:52.688 --> 00:08:57.238
fewer last-minute crises and compliance slips, faster replies.

00:08:57.238 --> 00:09:01.300
Putting a number on even one of those turns a feeling into evidence.

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:04.900
First, we pick one or two measures that fit our role,

00:09:04.900 --> 00:09:08.825
like reply time, and start noting them, even informally.

00:09:08.825 --> 00:09:13.125
Finally, when we talk about our performance, we point to real changes.

00:09:13.875 --> 00:09:14.988
Picture this.

00:09:14.988 --> 00:09:19.538
Over a month, your reply time dropped from about 24 hours to 5.

00:09:19.538 --> 00:09:22.200
How do you present that in your review so it lands,

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:24.250
and what else would you put next to it?

00:09:26.175 --> 00:09:30.000
Section 3 of 6: Cognitive Load and Systems.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:33.288
We'll cover reducing the executive's cognitive load,

00:09:33.288 --> 00:09:36.200
building a system for anything that happens twice,

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:38.825
and file naming and version control.

00:09:40.750 --> 00:09:45.175
Every open-ended question we send an executive uses up some of his limited

00:09:45.175 --> 00:09:47.238
decision-making energy.

00:09:47.238 --> 00:09:50.550
Each 'What would you like to do?' costs him a little,

00:09:50.550 --> 00:09:54.650
and by midday there's nothing left for the decisions that really matter.

00:09:55.425 --> 00:09:58.413
So we do the thinking first: lay out the options,

00:09:58.413 --> 00:10:00.925
point out the trade-offs and check the risks.

00:10:00.925 --> 00:10:02.888
Then he only has to choose.

00:10:02.888 --> 00:10:05.875
A good line sounds like: 'There are three options;

00:10:05.875 --> 00:10:08.400
B fits best with this quarter's goals.'

00:10:10.300 --> 00:10:14.213
Before bringing him any decision, ask yourself what you'd recommend if it were your

00:10:14.213 --> 00:10:16.475
call, and bring that too.

00:10:17.250 --> 00:10:19.238
Here's a situation to work through.

00:10:19.238 --> 00:10:23.238
A colleague sends Elias: 'Option A: hotel near the court.

00:10:23.238 --> 00:10:25.663
Option B: hotel near the client.

00:10:25.663 --> 00:10:27.925
Option C: hotel near the airport.

00:10:27.925 --> 00:10:30.050
Let me know.' Elias sighs.

00:10:30.050 --> 00:10:32.550
What's missing, and how would you rewrite it?

00:10:34.450 --> 00:10:38.875
The second time you do something by hand is the signal to build a system for it,

00:10:38.875 --> 00:10:40.875
not the fifth or the tenth.

00:10:41.675 --> 00:10:44.350
Helpers solve the same problem over and over.

00:10:44.350 --> 00:10:48.400
Force multipliers solve it once, then build something so it never needs solving from

00:10:48.400 --> 00:10:49.550
scratch again.

00:10:49.550 --> 00:10:52.425
And a system doesn't have to be fancy: a checklist,

00:10:52.425 --> 00:10:55.013
a template or a simple tracker counts.

00:10:55.013 --> 00:11:00.288
Think of the weekly report, travel booking, contract approvals or investor updates.

00:11:00.288 --> 00:11:04.175
Each system has the same parts: the steps, what could be automated,

00:11:04.175 --> 00:11:07.075
where approval is needed and when to escalate.

00:11:07.850 --> 00:11:12.088
Think of one task you've done more than twice this month without a system.

00:11:12.088 --> 00:11:16.275
What would the first version look like if you built it in fifteen minutes?

00:11:18.175 --> 00:11:23.038
Without clear rules, a high-trust role quietly becomes a high-risk one.

00:11:23.038 --> 00:11:27.850
The same access that makes us valuable could cause real damage if nobody's keeping

00:11:27.850 --> 00:11:28.825
track.

00:11:29.625 --> 00:11:32.713
So a good assistant knows the rules they work within:

00:11:32.713 --> 00:11:36.275
what's business and what's personal, how much they can spend,

00:11:36.275 --> 00:11:38.725
when to escalate and who approves what.

00:11:38.725 --> 00:11:42.500
And they stay inside those rules on purpose, not by luck,

00:11:42.500 --> 00:11:45.925
with the limits written down somewhere they can check quickly.

00:11:46.725 --> 00:11:48.150
Now picture this.

00:11:48.150 --> 00:11:53.625
Six months later, finance questions a $3,000 personal expense you approved on

00:11:53.625 --> 00:11:55.288
Elias's behalf.

00:11:55.288 --> 00:11:58.700
What written records would protect you, and where should they be?

00:12:00.650 --> 00:12:04.438
In a law firm, the wrong version of a document can get signed,

00:12:04.438 --> 00:12:09.325
filed or sent to the other side, and file names are the first line of defense.

00:12:10.100 --> 00:12:13.650
A good file name tells you what the file is without opening it:

00:12:13.650 --> 00:12:17.125
the date, the matter, the document type and the version.

00:12:17.125 --> 00:12:21.150
And every document lives in one place, the firm's document system,

00:12:21.150 --> 00:12:24.500
not in copies scattered through email and desktops.

00:12:24.500 --> 00:12:26.150
One source of truth.

00:12:28.100 --> 00:12:31.025
First, one pattern for everything: year, month,

00:12:31.025 --> 00:12:35.325
day, then matter, document type and version, like 2026-10-02,

00:12:35.325 --> 00:12:38.013
Harlow, Engagement Letter, v03.

00:12:38.013 --> 00:12:40.263
Next, we never call a file 'final'.

00:12:40.263 --> 00:12:44.725
Drafts get version numbers, and the signed or filed copy is marked Executed or

00:12:44.725 --> 00:12:45.838
As-Filed.

00:12:45.838 --> 00:12:49.850
Then we save it in the matter's folder, and share a link instead of attaching it.

00:12:49.850 --> 00:12:54.175
Finally, when edits come back, we save a new version and keep the old ones.

00:12:54.950 --> 00:12:57.013
Here's a situation to work through.

00:12:57.013 --> 00:13:02.138
Elias asks you to send opposing counsel 'the final settlement agreement.' You find

00:13:02.138 --> 00:13:06.050
four files: 'Settlement final', 'Settlement final 2',

00:13:06.050 --> 00:13:11.375
'Settlement v5 EB edits', and a PDF called 'Settlement agreement final clean'.

00:13:11.375 --> 00:13:13.700
What do you do before sending anything?

00:13:15.625 --> 00:13:19.800
Section 4 of 6: The Legal EA Force Multiplier.

00:13:19.800 --> 00:13:23.100
Here we apply the force multiplier idea to legal work,

00:13:23.100 --> 00:13:27.725
from authority and boundaries to decision compression and risk buffering.

00:13:29.700 --> 00:13:33.100
Knowing what we can decide alone, and what needs approval,

00:13:33.100 --> 00:13:36.225
keeps a trusted role from becoming a risky one.

00:13:36.225 --> 00:13:40.375
A legal EA has extra layers on top of normal business rules,

00:13:40.375 --> 00:13:43.875
and mistakes there can have legal consequences for the firm.

00:13:44.675 --> 00:13:49.500
First, as a general EA, we politely refuse approvals we're not authorised for,

00:13:49.500 --> 00:13:52.063
get written confirmation for budget exceptions,

00:13:52.063 --> 00:13:56.725
write up verbal approvals afterwards and keep personal and company expenses strictly

00:13:56.725 --> 00:13:57.850
apart.

00:13:57.850 --> 00:14:03.063
Finally, as a legal EA, we refuse unauthorised requests for client files,

00:14:03.063 --> 00:14:06.563
insist on a conflict check before any new matter opens,

00:14:06.563 --> 00:14:10.888
escalate anything over our financial limit and follow client money rules without

00:14:10.888 --> 00:14:12.075
exception.

00:14:12.850 --> 00:14:14.162
Think about this one.

00:14:14.162 --> 00:14:20.975
Elias tells you in the corridor, 'Yes, approve the Harlow expert's extra $3,500.'

00:14:20.975 --> 00:14:22.625
Nothing is in writing.

00:14:22.625 --> 00:14:25.050
What do you do before the payment goes through?

00:14:26.975 --> 00:14:30.000
The best assistants stop problems before they happen,

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:32.813
and bring insight, not just information.

00:14:32.813 --> 00:14:38.050
That's two kinds of high-value work: catching problems early, and strategic support.

00:14:38.850 --> 00:14:43.400
First, a general EA catches risks like a problem name on a guest list,

00:14:43.400 --> 00:14:47.175
a contract about to renew or a typo in a press release.

00:14:47.175 --> 00:14:52.675
Next, a legal EA tracks compliance filing dates and discovery deadlines,

00:14:52.675 --> 00:14:57.300
checks signing rules on estate documents and catches unsigned engagement letters

00:14:57.300 --> 00:14:58.925
before work starts.

00:14:59.700 --> 00:15:04.900
Then, for strategic support, a general EA builds a dashboard of key numbers,

00:15:04.900 --> 00:15:09.575
writes a 30-day action summary after a board meeting and tracks follow-ups.

00:15:09.575 --> 00:15:13.788
Finally, a legal EA prepares summaries of legal exposure,

00:15:13.788 --> 00:15:18.525
organises case timelines to show gaps in evidence and builds templates that cut

00:15:18.525 --> 00:15:20.025
drafting errors.

00:15:20.775 --> 00:15:21.888
Picture this.

00:15:21.888 --> 00:15:26.900
Elias asks for 'a dashboard of the Meridian matter.' You could list every document

00:15:26.900 --> 00:15:30.762
and date, or build something that shows him what needs attention.

00:15:30.762 --> 00:15:32.925
Describe the version you'd build.

00:15:34.825 --> 00:15:39.225
The move from helper to force multiplier matters even more in legal work,

00:15:39.225 --> 00:15:44.075
because deadlines, liability and confidentiality raise the stakes.

00:15:44.850 --> 00:15:48.588
A traditional legal virtual assistant waits for instructions,

00:15:48.588 --> 00:15:51.888
finishes assigned tasks and formats documents.

00:15:51.888 --> 00:15:55.063
A legal force multiplier cuts through the complexity,

00:15:55.063 --> 00:16:00.125
spots legal risk before it bites, protects the attorney's time and helps decisions

00:16:00.125 --> 00:16:01.575
happen faster.

00:16:02.375 --> 00:16:05.263
First, we notice where our own work sits today:

00:16:05.263 --> 00:16:10.200
closer to formatting and finishing tasks, or closer to filtering and anticipating

00:16:10.200 --> 00:16:11.225
risk.

00:16:11.225 --> 00:16:15.338
Finally, we pick one regular legal task and deliberately move it the

00:16:15.338 --> 00:16:17.575
force-multiplier way this week.

00:16:18.350 --> 00:16:20.563
Here's a situation to think about.

00:16:20.563 --> 00:16:25.675
A discovery deadline was missed last month because 'nobody flagged it,' even though

00:16:25.675 --> 00:16:28.238
the notice was in the inbox you manage.

00:16:28.238 --> 00:16:31.450
What would a legal force multiplier have done differently?

00:16:33.375 --> 00:16:35.525
An attorney's head is already full.

00:16:35.525 --> 00:16:38.725
Our job is to take clutter away, not add to it.

00:16:39.475 --> 00:16:42.400
They're juggling case strategy, an emotional client,

00:16:42.400 --> 00:16:46.388
pressure to bring in money, compliance risk and court deadlines.

00:16:46.388 --> 00:16:50.238
Hand them forty-two unread emails, and we've added to the pile.

00:16:50.238 --> 00:16:53.525
Say 'three need your legal decision, two are billing approvals,

00:16:53.525 --> 00:16:56.425
and one is opposing counsel asking for more time',

00:16:56.425 --> 00:16:58.188
and we've taken a weight off.

00:16:58.188 --> 00:17:01.075
Same inbox, very different experience.

00:17:01.850 --> 00:17:02.963
Try this one.

00:17:02.963 --> 00:17:08.062
A colleague forwards Elias all 30 emails from a heated client thread 'so he has the

00:17:08.062 --> 00:17:11.925
full picture.' He asks you what the client actually wants.

00:17:11.925 --> 00:17:13.950
Write the summary you'd send.

00:17:15.900 --> 00:17:19.450
Anything that reaches the attorney should have earned their attention.

00:17:19.450 --> 00:17:24.725
Something can be urgent for admin, legal, money or reputation reasons.

00:17:24.725 --> 00:17:27.025
Our job is to tell the difference.

00:17:27.825 --> 00:17:30.950
First, we flag any risk to the statute of limitations,

00:17:30.950 --> 00:17:33.750
the legal deadline to file a claim, immediately,

00:17:33.750 --> 00:17:35.025
every time.

00:17:35.025 --> 00:17:39.275
Next, we let genuinely non-urgent things wait, like a newsletter draft,

00:17:39.275 --> 00:17:41.275
even if someone marked it urgent.

00:17:41.275 --> 00:17:45.550
Then, we escalate press enquiries about active cases straight away;

00:17:45.550 --> 00:17:48.550
they're a legal and a reputation risk at once.

00:17:48.550 --> 00:17:52.788
Finally, we sort client requests into those that need the attorney and those a

00:17:52.788 --> 00:17:54.750
standard reply can handle.

00:17:55.525 --> 00:17:57.663
Here's a situation to work through.

00:17:57.663 --> 00:18:02.762
A client emails in capitals, 'Urgent, urgent, call me now.' It's about parking at

00:18:02.762 --> 00:18:03.875
the courthouse.

00:18:03.875 --> 00:18:09.225
Meanwhile, a polite email from opposing counsel mentions 'the response due Friday.'

00:18:09.225 --> 00:18:10.700
How do you handle both?

00:18:12.625 --> 00:18:14.888
A helper asks what to do next.

00:18:14.888 --> 00:18:19.562
A force multiplier builds a system so the same crisis never happens again.

00:18:19.562 --> 00:18:24.475
In a law firm, systems don't just save time; they prevent malpractice.

00:18:24.475 --> 00:18:28.925
When deadlines live in someone's memory, something eventually slips through.

00:18:29.750 --> 00:18:33.200
First, we build a dashboard for litigation deadlines.

00:18:33.200 --> 00:18:37.050
Next, we write down a standard process for taking on a new client,

00:18:37.050 --> 00:18:40.500
so it doesn't depend on one person remembering the steps.

00:18:40.500 --> 00:18:44.525
Finally, we add a checklist for reconciling client trust money,

00:18:44.525 --> 00:18:48.750
consistent file names and a tracker for discovery responses.

00:18:49.525 --> 00:18:50.900
Then consider this.

00:18:50.900 --> 00:18:55.962
The team says fixing deadline tracking 'needs a new software purchase.' What could

00:18:55.962 --> 00:19:00.000
you build this week with a shared spreadsheet to solve most of the problem?

00:19:01.925 --> 00:19:06.112
Attorneys are paid for their judgment, so our job is to get them to the decision

00:19:06.112 --> 00:19:07.125
fast.

00:19:07.925 --> 00:19:09.362
Compare two emails.

00:19:09.362 --> 00:19:13.200
One says, 'Here's the 60-page contract.' The other says,

00:19:13.200 --> 00:19:17.075
'Three clauses differ from our standard: indemnification is wider,

00:19:17.075 --> 00:19:22.038
payment terms are down to ten days and the arbitration venue has moved to Texas.

00:19:22.038 --> 00:19:27.562
Please review sections 4, 7 and 11.' The second lets the attorney spend their time

00:19:27.562 --> 00:19:29.838
on the decision, not hunting for it.

00:19:29.838 --> 00:19:31.775
That's decision compression.

00:19:33.650 --> 00:19:38.050
Before sending anything long for review, ask yourself the three most important

00:19:38.050 --> 00:19:41.700
things the reviewer needs to know, and lead with those.

00:19:42.450 --> 00:19:44.138
Take a second with this one.

00:19:44.138 --> 00:19:50.325
A colleague forwards Elias a 90-page expert report with the note 'FYI.' Elias asks,

00:19:50.325 --> 00:19:53.350
'What do I need from this?' What should the note have said?

00:19:55.250 --> 00:19:59.525
Protecting the attorney from avoidable mistakes is one of the most trusted things we

00:19:59.525 --> 00:20:03.175
do, and every risk we catch builds credibility.

00:20:03.925 --> 00:20:05.450
The habits are simple.

00:20:05.450 --> 00:20:08.762
We track compliance deadlines in every jurisdiction,

00:20:08.762 --> 00:20:11.900
check signatures and witnesses as a standard step,

00:20:11.900 --> 00:20:16.225
protect privileged information, write down approvals and watch trust account

00:20:16.225 --> 00:20:17.425
procedures.

00:20:17.425 --> 00:20:20.462
And no engagement letter means no billable work,

00:20:20.462 --> 00:20:23.300
even for a client who's been with us for years.

00:20:24.100 --> 00:20:25.425
Now picture this.

00:20:25.425 --> 00:20:28.950
It's 4:50 p.m., a will signing is about to happen,

00:20:28.950 --> 00:20:32.738
but only one witness is present and the state requires two.

00:20:32.738 --> 00:20:35.850
Everyone wants to 'just get it done.' What do you do?

00:20:37.800 --> 00:20:41.850
Section 5 of 6: Leveraging AI with Precision.

00:20:41.850 --> 00:20:45.375
We'll start with the rules for AI use in the legal industry,

00:20:45.375 --> 00:20:50.375
then what a large language model is, and how to pick the right tool for each job.

00:20:52.325 --> 00:20:57.200
Before we talk about what AI can do, let's agree the standard it always has to meet.

00:20:57.200 --> 00:20:58.600
Three rules.

00:20:59.400 --> 00:21:02.538
First, the firm's and the attorney's preferences come first;

00:21:02.538 --> 00:21:07.050
some are happy with AI, some aren't, and some only allow it for certain tasks.

00:21:07.050 --> 00:21:10.962
Second, anything involving legal judgment stays with a human.

00:21:10.962 --> 00:21:14.200
AI can help draft, but the attorney makes the call.

00:21:14.200 --> 00:21:16.512
And third, confidentiality.

00:21:16.512 --> 00:21:21.100
Pasting privileged client information into the wrong tool isn't a small slip;

00:21:21.100 --> 00:21:23.175
it can be a real legal problem.

00:21:23.950 --> 00:21:29.075
So before using AI on any task, check what this firm and this attorney allow on this

00:21:29.075 --> 00:21:32.700
matter, and check whether what you're pasting is privileged.

00:21:34.600 --> 00:21:37.338
If you're not sure it's appropriate, ask first,

00:21:37.338 --> 00:21:39.800
rather than asking forgiveness later.

00:21:39.800 --> 00:21:43.450
These rules are a filter every AI technique must pass,

00:21:43.450 --> 00:21:45.600
not a disclaimer we read once.

00:21:46.375 --> 00:21:47.988
Here's one to think about.

00:21:47.988 --> 00:21:53.113
Elias asks you to 'run the Meridian deposition transcript through AI and pull out

00:21:53.113 --> 00:21:57.325
the contradictions.' Before you do anything, what do you need to check,

00:21:57.325 --> 00:21:58.975
and what do you say to him?

00:21:59.725 --> 00:22:02.725
And what if another partner on a shared matter has said,

00:22:02.725 --> 00:22:05.075
'No AI on my matters, ever'?

00:22:07.100 --> 00:22:10.888
Three digital skills together take repetitive work off our plate:

00:22:10.888 --> 00:22:15.225
using AI properly, simple automation and clear charts.

00:22:16.025 --> 00:22:19.500
Using AI properly means real work from start to finish,

00:22:19.500 --> 00:22:24.025
like a full first draft, or turning a 50-page report into a five-point morning

00:22:24.025 --> 00:22:27.138
brief, not just playing with clever prompts.

00:22:27.138 --> 00:22:30.725
Automation means little rules so things happen by themselves,

00:22:30.725 --> 00:22:33.950
like a task appearing when a certain client emails.

00:22:33.950 --> 00:22:38.788
And a basic chart in Excel or Tableau shows a budget far more clearly than a table

00:22:38.788 --> 00:22:39.925
of numbers.

00:22:41.875 --> 00:22:46.950
First, we pick one repeating task and automate it, even with just an email rule.

00:22:46.950 --> 00:22:49.638
Next, we learn a basic charting tool.

00:22:49.638 --> 00:22:53.200
Then, we use the three skills together, not just one.

00:22:53.200 --> 00:22:56.825
Finally, once a month, we look for the next thing to automate.

00:22:57.575 --> 00:22:59.163
Take a second with this one.

00:22:59.163 --> 00:23:03.250
Every Friday, someone asks, 'Where are we on the billing numbers?',

00:23:03.250 --> 00:23:05.588
and you spend an hour pulling them together.

00:23:05.588 --> 00:23:09.350
What digital fix would stop that fire from starting each week?

00:23:11.275 --> 00:23:14.425
An AI chatbot predicts the next likely word.

00:23:14.425 --> 00:23:18.388
It doesn't look anything up, so sounding confident tells us nothing about whether

00:23:18.388 --> 00:23:19.500
it's right.

00:23:20.275 --> 00:23:24.563
That's the most important thing to understand about tools like ChatGPT,

00:23:24.563 --> 00:23:26.413
Claude or Gemini.

00:23:26.413 --> 00:23:29.200
They learned patterns from huge amounts of text,

00:23:29.200 --> 00:23:32.088
and produce the words most likely to come next.

00:23:32.088 --> 00:23:34.275
That's why they write so fluently.

00:23:34.275 --> 00:23:38.650
It's also why they sometimes invent a fact, a date or a legal case,

00:23:38.650 --> 00:23:41.975
with exactly the same confidence as something true.

00:23:42.775 --> 00:23:46.500
So we check every specific fact, date or figure ourselves,

00:23:46.500 --> 00:23:50.775
especially anything going to a client, a court or the executive.

00:23:52.725 --> 00:23:56.113
Made-up answers tend to appear where the tool doesn't really know,

00:23:56.113 --> 00:23:58.850
filling the gap smoothly instead of admitting it.

00:23:58.850 --> 00:24:04.625
So 'how would I check this?' becomes an automatic question for every output we use.

00:24:05.400 --> 00:24:07.613
Here's a situation to work through.

00:24:07.613 --> 00:24:12.875
An AI draft of a client letter confidently says a filing deadline is 'within 21

00:24:12.875 --> 00:24:16.750
days.' The actual rule for this court is 14.

00:24:16.750 --> 00:24:20.425
What does that tell you, and what's your checking habit from now on?

00:24:22.325 --> 00:24:23.025
Quick check.

00:24:23.025 --> 00:24:27.813
True or false: an LLM looks up facts in a database before answering.

00:24:27.813 --> 00:24:29.100
Take a second.

00:24:29.850 --> 00:24:31.500
The answer is false.

00:24:31.500 --> 00:24:34.563
It generates a statistically likely response.

00:24:34.563 --> 00:24:38.800
Anything true in that response is true because the pattern happened to match

00:24:38.800 --> 00:24:41.425
reality, not because it was looked up.

00:24:43.350 --> 00:24:48.038
A few AI terms come up all the time, and knowing them helps us avoid the most common

00:24:48.038 --> 00:24:49.100
mistakes.

00:24:49.825 --> 00:24:53.963
A prompt is our instruction; a better prompt gets a better answer.

00:24:53.963 --> 00:24:58.613
A hallucination is when the tool states something false with complete confidence.

00:24:58.613 --> 00:25:03.125
The context window is its working memory; paste in too much and it gets fuzzy

00:25:03.125 --> 00:25:04.400
towards the end.

00:25:04.400 --> 00:25:09.225
And tokens are the chunks of text it counts, roughly three-quarters of a word each,

00:25:09.225 --> 00:25:12.050
which is why long documents can hit a limit.

00:25:13.975 --> 00:25:17.125
In practice, keep what you paste to a sensible length,

00:25:17.125 --> 00:25:20.663
and remember token limits are real on longer documents,

00:25:20.663 --> 00:25:22.675
even on premium accounts.

00:25:24.600 --> 00:25:28.425
Treat every output as something to check, not something to trust.

00:25:29.250 --> 00:25:31.363
Now a situation to think about.

00:25:31.363 --> 00:25:36.200
A colleague pastes a client's full name, medical details and settlement amount into

00:25:36.200 --> 00:25:40.500
a free AI account to 'tidy up the wording.' What's the risk,

00:25:40.500 --> 00:25:42.125
and what should she have done?

00:25:44.025 --> 00:25:44.725
Quick check.

00:25:44.725 --> 00:25:48.975
You paste a 40-page contract and ask for a summary of section 12.

00:25:48.975 --> 00:25:52.275
The AI's answer mixes up details from section 3.

00:25:52.275 --> 00:25:53.750
What most likely happened?

00:25:53.750 --> 00:25:58.525
Is it A, context window strain, where it lost precision across a long document?

00:25:58.525 --> 00:26:02.425
B, section 12 was too complex for the AI to understand?

00:26:02.425 --> 00:26:05.000
C, the contract format wasn't supported?

00:26:05.000 --> 00:26:07.575
Or D, the AI refused the request?

00:26:07.575 --> 00:26:08.850
Take a second.

00:26:09.600 --> 00:26:12.550
The answer is A, context window strain.

00:26:12.550 --> 00:26:15.838
When a document is long, precision degrades across it,

00:26:15.838 --> 00:26:19.450
so paste just the relevant section when precision matters.

00:26:21.400 --> 00:26:25.638
AI can do three quite different jobs, and choosing the right one avoids the

00:26:25.638 --> 00:26:27.300
costliest mistakes.

00:26:28.050 --> 00:26:31.563
The first is creating new text, like a polite decline,

00:26:31.563 --> 00:26:34.313
a bio or a summary of a messy thread.

00:26:34.313 --> 00:26:38.000
The second is pulling out exact information without changing it,

00:26:38.000 --> 00:26:41.925
like every date or action item in a long email chain.

00:26:41.925 --> 00:26:44.600
And the third is connecting a trigger to an action,

00:26:44.600 --> 00:26:49.800
like filing attachments automatically or alerting us when a VIP emails.

00:26:51.750 --> 00:26:56.525
First, before opening a tool, decide which of the three jobs the task needs.

00:26:56.525 --> 00:27:01.938
Then, for exact facts, use extraction, because a generative tool might subtly change

00:27:01.938 --> 00:27:03.125
a detail.

00:27:05.050 --> 00:27:08.975
After that, to connect a trigger to an action, use an automation tool,

00:27:08.975 --> 00:27:10.225
not a chatbot.

00:27:10.225 --> 00:27:14.463
Finally, if you're unsure and exact facts or figures are involved,

00:27:14.463 --> 00:27:16.063
choose extraction.

00:27:16.063 --> 00:27:19.075
A changed detail is the costlier mistake.

00:27:19.850 --> 00:27:24.713
Try sorting three tasks: pull every deadline out of a 20-email thread,

00:27:24.713 --> 00:27:28.725
draft a thank-you note to a client, and get an alert whenever opposing counsel

00:27:28.725 --> 00:27:29.838
emails.

00:27:29.838 --> 00:27:31.575
Which mode fits each?

00:27:33.525 --> 00:27:37.763
The three big AI tools have different strengths, but the same risks.

00:27:37.763 --> 00:27:41.400
All three can invent facts, all three have memory limits,

00:27:41.400 --> 00:27:45.350
and all three may learn from what we paste, depending on the account.

00:27:46.100 --> 00:27:48.538
Beyond that, they suit different jobs.

00:27:48.538 --> 00:27:52.975
Claude tends to handle long documents well and lets us control the tone.

00:27:52.975 --> 00:27:57.850
ChatGPT has the widest range of add-ons and is quick for trying drafts.

00:27:57.850 --> 00:28:03.475
Gemini lives inside Gmail, Docs and Calendar, so it works with live information,

00:28:03.475 --> 00:28:06.600
which means our security habits matter even more.

00:28:08.500 --> 00:28:11.675
Whichever tool you use, the same checks apply.

00:28:11.675 --> 00:28:14.425
And when you're automating inside Google Workspace,

00:28:14.425 --> 00:28:19.300
start simple, like Gmail filters, before reaching for anything advanced.

00:28:21.225 --> 00:28:23.250
Then pick the tool for the job.

00:28:23.250 --> 00:28:28.650
For quick drafting and trying versions, ChatGPT is often the practical choice.

00:28:28.650 --> 00:28:32.388
For anything working on live email, documents or calendars,

00:28:32.388 --> 00:28:35.575
Gemini fits, with stricter security habits.

00:28:36.325 --> 00:28:38.538
Here's a situation to think about.

00:28:38.538 --> 00:28:43.450
A colleague built a complex script that automatically files client emails into Drive

00:28:43.450 --> 00:28:44.525
folders.

00:28:44.525 --> 00:28:47.388
She's leaving, and nobody else understands it.

00:28:47.388 --> 00:28:51.925
What simpler automation could replace it, and why is simpler better here?

00:28:53.850 --> 00:28:56.900
Section 6 of 6: Email Management.

00:28:56.900 --> 00:29:00.925
We'll treat email as a control system, then look at inbox triage,

00:29:00.925 --> 00:29:03.875
the daily routine and the priority matrix.

00:29:05.775 --> 00:29:10.225
Before we act on any email for the executive, we need to know exactly what we're

00:29:10.225 --> 00:29:11.575
allowed to do.

00:29:12.375 --> 00:29:14.325
There are three levels of access.

00:29:14.325 --> 00:29:18.850
With full access, we can read, reply, file and send in his name.

00:29:18.850 --> 00:29:23.600
With draft and review, we prepare replies, and he approves them before they go.

00:29:23.600 --> 00:29:27.625
With triage only, we sort and flag, and he replies himself.

00:29:27.625 --> 00:29:30.800
Using a higher level than we've been given isn't helpful.

00:29:30.800 --> 00:29:32.600
It's crossing a line.

00:29:34.550 --> 00:29:38.850
First, confirm your level, and don't assume it's the same as your last job.

00:29:38.850 --> 00:29:43.750
Then, on draft and review, wait for his OK, even if you're sure it's right.

00:29:45.675 --> 00:29:50.225
After that, on triage only, resist drafting replies he didn't ask for.

00:29:50.225 --> 00:29:54.075
Finally, if your level isn't clear, ask rather than guess.

00:29:54.850 --> 00:29:55.850
Think about this.

00:29:55.850 --> 00:29:57.788
You're on draft and review.

00:29:57.788 --> 00:30:02.800
A client emails at 6 p.m. asking to confirm tomorrow's 9 a.m. meeting,

00:30:02.800 --> 00:30:04.713
and Elias is on a flight.

00:30:04.713 --> 00:30:07.575
What can you do now, and what has to wait for him?

00:30:09.500 --> 00:30:14.200
Top assistants handle eighty to ninety percent of routine emails themselves,

00:30:14.200 --> 00:30:16.225
without sending them up the line.

00:30:17.000 --> 00:30:20.213
That can sound scary, but think about a typical inbox:

00:30:20.213 --> 00:30:24.325
confirmations, scheduling, simple questions, updates.

00:30:24.325 --> 00:30:27.288
Most of it doesn't need the executive personally.

00:30:27.288 --> 00:30:31.538
The first step is honest: find out how much we handle ourselves today.

00:30:31.538 --> 00:30:34.775
Then we close the gap one kind of email at a time.

00:30:36.725 --> 00:30:40.950
First, spot the routine emails you're still escalating out of habit.

00:30:40.950 --> 00:30:42.913
Those are the quickest wins.

00:30:42.913 --> 00:30:46.713
Then build the judgment to handle them, checking your calls against what he'd

00:30:46.713 --> 00:30:48.325
actually have wanted.

00:30:50.275 --> 00:30:53.825
After that, as you handle more, guard the two big risks:

00:30:53.825 --> 00:30:57.975
never miss a critical deadline, and never breach confidentiality.

00:30:57.975 --> 00:31:01.613
Finally, keep the inbox at or near zero every day,

00:31:01.613 --> 00:31:04.050
as visible proof the system's working.

00:31:04.800 --> 00:31:06.975
Here's a situation to work through.

00:31:06.975 --> 00:31:11.550
It's going well, and then you notice an email with a filing deadline buried in a

00:31:11.550 --> 00:31:13.938
thread you archived as routine.

00:31:13.938 --> 00:31:16.575
What changes in your triage from now on?

00:31:18.500 --> 00:31:23.088
A simple routine at the start, middle and end of the day means nothing important

00:31:23.088 --> 00:31:27.650
slips through the cracks, and the executive starts every day informed instead of

00:31:27.650 --> 00:31:28.825
surprised.

00:31:29.625 --> 00:31:34.163
First, the morning scan: 15 to 30 minutes to spot anything urgent,

00:31:34.163 --> 00:31:36.925
clear the junk and prepare a short briefing.

00:31:36.925 --> 00:31:41.625
Next, the midday review: draft replies, chase anything waiting and confirm the

00:31:41.625 --> 00:31:43.275
afternoon's meetings.

00:31:43.275 --> 00:31:48.650
Finally, the end-of-day review: nothing urgent left hanging, and tomorrow set up.

00:31:49.450 --> 00:31:51.413
Now build it around Elias.

00:31:51.413 --> 00:31:56.650
He's in court by 9 a.m. three days a week and checks messages at 12:30 during the

00:31:56.650 --> 00:31:57.850
lunch break.

00:31:57.850 --> 00:32:02.200
When do your check-ins happen on those days, and what's ready for him at each one?

00:32:04.125 --> 00:32:08.225
The morning briefing replaces the inbox; if it's as long as the inbox,

00:32:08.225 --> 00:32:09.513
it isn't working.

00:32:09.513 --> 00:32:11.875
Picture Elias with his first coffee.

00:32:11.875 --> 00:32:13.800
He doesn't want 30 emails.

00:32:13.800 --> 00:32:16.413
He wants to know what needs him today, in a minute.

00:32:16.413 --> 00:32:18.425
The inbox, digested.

00:32:19.225 --> 00:32:22.975
First, read the whole inbox yourself, but never just forward it.

00:32:22.975 --> 00:32:28.000
Next, boil each item down to one line: what it is and where it stands.

00:32:28.000 --> 00:32:32.750
Then, put the most urgent first, like an approval that expires today.

00:32:34.675 --> 00:32:37.250
After that, keep it to a handful of lines.

00:32:37.250 --> 00:32:40.500
Finally, send it at the same time every morning.

00:32:41.250 --> 00:32:42.888
Here's one to work through.

00:32:42.888 --> 00:32:47.525
Elias says he stopped reading your briefings because they're 'as long as my inbox.'

00:32:47.525 --> 00:32:50.988
Rewrite yesterday's briefing in three parts: needs you,

00:32:50.988 --> 00:32:52.675
handled and heads-up.

00:32:54.575 --> 00:32:55.300
Quick check.

00:32:55.300 --> 00:32:59.100
What best distinguishes high-performing email management from average?

00:32:59.100 --> 00:33:02.650
Is it A, forwarding every email so nothing is missed?

00:33:02.650 --> 00:33:05.638
B, deleting anything that looks unimportant?

00:33:05.638 --> 00:33:08.725
C, replying to everything within 5 minutes?

00:33:08.725 --> 00:33:13.300
Or D, handling 80 to 90 percent of operational emails independently,

00:33:13.300 --> 00:33:15.188
with zero missed deadlines?

00:33:15.188 --> 00:33:16.475
Take a second.

00:33:17.225 --> 00:33:17.725
It's D.

00:33:17.613 --> 00:33:21.525
It's about independent, reliable handling of the bulk of email,

00:33:21.525 --> 00:33:24.075
not speed or forwarding volume.

00:33:26.000 --> 00:33:30.275
Deciding how urgent something is comes first, because that decides how fast you

00:33:30.275 --> 00:33:31.425
respond.

00:33:32.200 --> 00:33:34.800
Everything falls into one of two top groups.

00:33:34.800 --> 00:33:38.150
The first can't wait: legal deadlines, major clients,

00:33:38.150 --> 00:33:41.375
the press, money approvals and genuine crises.

00:33:41.375 --> 00:33:43.888
Those go to the executive right away.

00:33:43.888 --> 00:33:47.325
The second is important, but not worth interrupting him for:

00:33:47.325 --> 00:33:51.125
new business, partnerships, board matters and vendor deals.

00:33:51.125 --> 00:33:55.125
For those, we have a drafted response ready within a few hours.

00:33:57.075 --> 00:34:00.000
First, decide the tier before you do anything else.

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:04.650
Next, Tier 1 goes to the executive straight away, no exceptions.

00:34:04.650 --> 00:34:08.675
Then, Tier 2 gets a response within two to four hours.

00:34:10.600 --> 00:34:14.713
After that, if you can't tell the tier, treat it as Tier 1 until you know.

00:34:14.713 --> 00:34:17.925
Over-escalating costs less than missing something.

00:34:17.925 --> 00:34:22.725
Finally, look back at your calls now and then, and learn from the wrong ones.

00:34:23.500 --> 00:34:24.713
Speed round.

00:34:24.713 --> 00:34:27.688
Call the tier for each: a court clerk's notice.

00:34:27.688 --> 00:34:29.275
A partnership inquiry.

00:34:29.275 --> 00:34:31.388
The bar association newsletter.

00:34:31.388 --> 00:34:33.588
A reporter asking for comment.

00:34:33.588 --> 00:34:36.875
A vendor saying an invoice is 60 days overdue.

00:34:37.675 --> 00:34:38.775
And one more.

00:34:38.775 --> 00:34:43.113
The monthly email from the cleaning company normally goes in Tier 3.

00:34:43.113 --> 00:34:47.700
This month it says they found an unlocked filing cabinet full of client files after

00:34:47.700 --> 00:34:48.713
hours.

00:34:48.713 --> 00:34:51.250
What tier is it now, and what do you do?

00:34:53.175 --> 00:34:53.900
Quick check.

00:34:53.900 --> 00:34:57.313
A media inquiry with a deadline tomorrow just landed.

00:34:57.313 --> 00:35:01.575
Under the Priority Matrix, is it A, something to batch with the newsletter?

00:35:01.575 --> 00:35:03.338
B, not worth flagging?

00:35:03.338 --> 00:35:06.725
C, Tier 1, notify the executive immediately?

00:35:06.725 --> 00:35:10.588
Or D, Tier 2, draft a response in two to four hours?

00:35:10.588 --> 00:35:11.875
Take a second.

00:35:12.625 --> 00:35:13.125
It's C.

00:35:12.950 --> 00:35:18.625
Media inquiries with a hard deadline sit squarely in Tier 1: immediate escalation.

00:35:20.600 --> 00:35:22.088
Now it's your turn.

00:35:22.088 --> 00:35:26.763
In the Force Multiplier Challenge, you'll anticipate what a vague executive request

00:35:26.763 --> 00:35:31.538
really needs, write a prompt that actually runs and produces real output,

00:35:31.538 --> 00:35:35.663
handle a messy combined scenario, then clear a real inbox,

00:35:35.663 --> 00:35:39.975
building your own filing system and catching a hidden phishing attempt.

